## Relations

- A principal owns its personal rooms⟦f:work-rooms⟧ and any number of other rooms⟦/f⟧; an agent's
  principal follows from its node.
- A run has its personal-room seat from its first event⟦f:work-rooms⟧, plus a further run seat
  per room it joined or created⟦/f⟧, a new one for each seat key minted anew, and
  the seat ingest starts beside any of them (Run seat).
- A restore block carries the qualifying pins of ⟦f:work-rooms⟧every room its run has had a
  seat in during the run, ⟦/f⟧its run's personal room⟦f:work-rooms⟧ included⟦/f⟧ (PIN-10)⟦f:work-rooms⟧; a leave⟦f:moderation⟧ or a kick⟦/f⟧⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧ or
  a bar⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧ keeps them⟦f:shared-rooms⟧, but once the room is foreign only those Foreign room names⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧.
- Ingest splits one transcript by run.
- Every seat belongs to one principal and one room; every writer to one seat.
  A seat certified by this node's device key⟦f:access-tokens⟧ or token key⟦/f⟧⟦f:node-clone⟧, or by the device key of
  any node its home descends from by node clone⟦f:store-protection⟧ or backup restore⟦/f⟧, however far
  back, whether or not a lineage acceptance stands (Node),⟦/f⟧⟦f:paired-phone⟧ or a
  paired phone's device seat in the personal room of the node it pairs with,⟦/f⟧
  belongs to this node's principal; any other seat⟦f:principal-keys⟧, until PRV-10
  ships,⟦/f⟧ is kept as another node's or principal's, attributed to its seat key
  and untrusted (I8)⟦f:principal-keys⟧; once PRV-10
  ships, it belongs to the principal its key chains to (Principal), and is kept
  as theirs when that is another principal⟦/f⟧.
- Each event goes to exactly one seat's writer. A run's event other than a
  room act goes to ⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:git-tracking⟧its run
  seat in the room it works in at that moment, one it joined or created that
  names the branch of the run's latest preceding event naming its branch⟦f:shared-rooms⟧ by a
  branch link its join or create room act covers (Join)⟦/f⟧, of two such rooms the one whose branch link
  came first in causal order, concurrent ones by the lower commitment, while
  that seat is a member and has work (its role⟦f:moderation⟧, no mute⟦/f⟧), per the room state
  this node holds when it records the event (LANE-01, LANE-16); else to ⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧its
  personal-room seat; what `parinn ingest` appends for a witnessed run goes
  instead to the seat ingest starts beside that run seat (Run seat). A room act
  goes to the writer of the seat that signs it. A principal act⟦f:principal-keys⟧ or an expire act⟦/f⟧
  goes to the device seat of the device that ⟦f:principal-keys⟧signs it, or before PRV-10 ships
  ⟦/f⟧records it, in the room it acts on⟦f:work-rooms⟧; a node of a principal with a member seat
  there first joins it⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧ without admission⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧. One that acts on no room⟦f:work-rooms⟧ or on a room
  its principal has no member seat in⟦/f⟧⟦f:paired-phone⟧, or that a paired phone signs,⟦/f⟧ goes to
  that device seat in the personal room⟦f:work-rooms⟧, naming the room, and that room shows it
  by address⟦f:conversation⟧ as it shows a cross-room post⟦/f⟧ (LANE-29); for a room with no member
  seat, the room merge counting it rests on OQ-41⟦/f⟧. A seat key's rotation goes
  to that seat's own writer (SEC-27).⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:purge-retention⟧ A tombstone⟦f:peer-sync⟧, and an erasure request a
  retention policy sends (PEER-11),⟦/f⟧ goes where the recording device's principal act
  on the room it names would go.⟦/f⟧⟦f:launcher⟧⟦f:git-tracking⟧ The launcher's event about a room's branch⟦f:bridges⟧,
  and a bridge's,⟦/f⟧ goes where the recording device's principal act on that room
  would go.⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧ Any other event with no run goes to the recording device's seat in the
  personal room of its node⟦f:paired-phone⟧, or for a paired phone, of the node it pairs with⟦/f⟧.
- A run's history spans its seats' writers, tied together by the run.⟦f:peer-sync⟧ Peers
  exchange segments, so a room contains only the events routed to its seats and
  shows others by address (LANE-01).⟦/f⟧
- ⟦f:work-rooms⟧Principals and agents create rooms; an agent's room is owned by its principal.
  ⟦/f⟧ParleyInn never creates a room on its own initiative; a node's personal room comes
  from the principal installing ParleyInn there (Personal room)⟦f:node-clone⟧, or is carried over
  to a node clone⟦/f⟧⟦f:work-rooms⟧, and ParleyInn may suggest a room or a join⟦/f⟧.
- A pin naming no room belongs to its author's principal's personal room on the
  node that wrote it.
- Recall extends only to the principal's
  rooms the agent has a seat in⟦f:conversation⟧⟦f:work-rooms⟧ and the cross-room posts they show⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:shared-rooms⟧, never to a
  foreign room unless the call names it⟦/f⟧, though the `run` scope always reaches
  every writer of the run's own seats⟦/f⟧.

## Names follow the model

Code, CLI verbs, MCP tools, settings keys, events, documentation, UI copy,
errors and logs use the model's words.

- An MCP tool for a room act an agent takes is `room_<act>`⟦f:work-rooms⟧, but `room_create`
  for create room⟦/f⟧.
  Every other MCP tool is `<concept>_<verb>`, with the reading verbs `get`,
  `list`, `search` and `expand`.
- A CLI command that acts on a concept is `parinn <concept> <verb>`. Node-wide
  utilities keep one word: `install`, `uninstall`, `status`, `doctor`, `verify`,
  `rebuild`, `migrate`, `ingest`⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧, `import`⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧, `export`⟦/f⟧⟦f:purge-retention⟧, `purge`⟦/f⟧, `audit`,
  `canary`⟦f:work-rooms⟧, `why`⟦/f⟧⟦f:browser-room-view⟧, `open`⟦/f⟧, `hook`, `mcp`⟦f:compute-kernel⟧ and `kernel-worker`⟦/f⟧.⟦f:launcher⟧
  `parinn launch` is the launcher's entry point, not a CLI command.⟦/f⟧⟦f:browser-room-view⟧
  `parinn ui` is the room-view component's entry point, not a CLI command.⟦/f⟧
- An option that selects a concept is named for it: `--author`, `--seat`,
  `--writer`, `--run`, `--room`.
- A **settings key** names one setting in a settings layer:
  `<concept>.<setting>`, or `<concept>.<room>.<setting>` or
  `<concept>.<room>.<qualifier>` per room, the concept singular and in snake
  case (`node.deployment_mode`). A multi-word concept is snake case in MCP tools
  and settings keys, kebab case in CLI commands.
- Layout words (such as tab, panel, pane, gutter, sheet, stack, tile, pill,
  rail, composer and command palette) name parts of a screen, never concepts.
- ParleyInn defines no slash commands. A harness skill may call MCP tools or the
  CLI; a skill's call is the agent's own tool call, never a principal act.

## Not ParleyInn concepts

| Term                                                                              | Why                                                                                            | Where else it may appear                                                                                                 |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| session                                                                           | It belongs to the harness; ParleyInn speaks of runs.                                               | As "harness session", or inside a harness's own names, such as `SessionStart`, `session_id` and `allow-session`.         |
⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:git-tracking⟧| project                                                                           | A room names branches in repositories; nothing is scoped to a project.                         | The harness's `~/.claude/projects` path, the harness settings scope `--scope project`, and ParleyInn's own software project. |
⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧| tenant                                                                            | Replaced by principal.                                                                         | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦f:moderation⟧| operator, as a role or a person                                                   | The role is moderator; the person running a node is the node's principal.                      | Only as the `operator` provenance class.                                                                                 |
⟦/f⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧| owner act, owner key, owner surface                                               | Replaced by principal act, principal key and principal surface.                                | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦f:service-accounts⟧⟦f:bundle-import⟧| bot, co-author, actor, poster, bound human                                        | Replaced by service account, pull-request author, author (for actor and poster) and principal. | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧| writer key, writer certificate                                                    | Replaced by seat key and seat certificate.                                                     | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:git-tracking⟧⟦f:moderation⟧⟦f:publishing⟧| attempt, claim of work, read only (a seat state)                                  | Replaced by branch, `stake` pin and mute.                                                      | Nowhere else; "read-only" as an ordinary adjective (read-only publishing) stays.                                         |
⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:launcher⟧⟦f:results-and-verdicts⟧⟦f:witness-check⟧| run component; own run and witness run as evidence classes                        | Replaced by launcher, own check and witness check.                                             | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:away-policy⟧⟦f:room-view-surfaces⟧| away mode, room alias, child agent                                                | Replaced by away policy, petname and "a subagent⟦f:delegation-grants⟧ or a delegate⟦/f⟧".                               | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧| trusted boundary                                                                  | Replaced by trusted sources; "boundary" means a network boundary.                              | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦f:conversation⟧| room board                                                                        | Replaced by conversation.                                                                      | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧| participant, player                                                               | Replaced by seat and member.                                                                   | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
| lane                                                                              | Renamed to room.                                                                               | LANE requirement ids and file names such as lane-view.feature.                                                           |
⟦f:results-and-verdicts⟧| judge, approval gate                                                              | Removed by the stakeholder; a person records a verdict.                                        | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦f:service-accounts⟧| service-account seat                                                              | Replaced by device seat.                                                                       | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧⟦f:peer-sync⟧| import of a transcript or a peer's segments; imported run                         | Only a bundle is imported.                                                                     | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:presence-proof⟧⟦f:access-tokens⟧⟦f:needs-you⟧⟦f:room-status⟧| presence check; unqualified token; Needs you as a run or room status; work marker | Replaced by presence proof, access token or model token, Asking, and ingest marker.            | Nowhere else.                                                                                                            |
⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧| hide                                                                              | Removed by the stakeholder; say the UI does not show it.                                       | The cryptographic term "hiding commitment" (REC-17).                                                                     |

Every excluded term may still appear in **historical records**: the SRS change
log, accepted ADRs and plan records, which keep the words of their time.
Excluded words, and words the model defines⟦f:results-and-verdicts⟧, such as `verdict`⟦/f⟧, may also appear
in that tool's meaning in files an outside tool writes and maintains, or that
name its fields. Examples are frit's plan skills and plan files, and the
repository's own engineering tooling, such as ENG-28's review gate and the
requirement naming its fields. The domain-model agent skips them.

## Changing the model

A new concept, a renamed one or a new relation is a stakeholder decision. It
lands here before anything uses it. The domain-model agent reviews every change
to these files and every proposal to change it. It names the invariants whose
wording would change, and every use the change makes stale or that runs ahead.
